 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Where it is used",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "One part covers one data line against ESD and surge. Being bidirectional it clamps both positive and negative excursions, so the protected signal may swing either side of ground. It is not intended for a line that sits on a DC supply rail. Typical uses include data lines in computers, peripherals, communication gear and audio-video equipment."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Surge rating",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Surge figures use the IEC 61000-4-5 8/20 µs current pulse: the current peaks at 8 µs and has fallen to half its peak by 20 µs, decaying exponentially. At 3 A the part clamps to 70 V at most. Peak pulse power falls as the pulse lengthens, from about 1.2 kW at 1 µs to 10 W near 4 ms, and with temperature it holds its 25 °C value up to 25 °C then falls in a straight line to zero at 150 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD rating and test pulse",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Contact discharge to IEC 61000-4-2 is rated to 23 kV (level 4), human body model to MIL-STD-883 to 10 kV, each with ten pulses. The IEC 61000-4-2 current pulse rises in 0.6 ns to 1 ns to its first peak; its later levels are taken at 30 ns and 60 ns."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Clamping under ESD",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "With a 1 kV strike from the IEC 61000-4-2 network, the unclamped line voltage jumps to roughly 900 V and decays to about 50 V some 450 ns after the strike. With the diode fitted the voltage steps to about 27 V, holds near 25 V to 27 V for about 200 ns, then falls back to zero within some 40 ns. A -1 kV strike gives the mirror image."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitance",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Capacitance is highest at zero bias, 11 pF typical, and falls with reverse voltage, to about 6.4 pF at 10 V and 5.1 pF at 24 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Board layout",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Layout decides how well ESD, fast transients and surge are suppressed. Put the diode right at the connector or input terminal, with a short tie to the protected line and a short return to ground. Keep loops of every kind small, power and ground included. Do not route protected traces beside unprotected ones, and keep parallel signal runs few. Give each protector its own return to ground rather than a shared one. Use a ground plane where possible, with ground vias on multilayer boards."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "SOD323 (JEITA SC-76), two leads, body 1.7 mm x 1.25 mm x 0.95 mm, 1.3 mm pitch. A marking bar on the body marks the pin 1 (K1) end."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Qualification",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The part is not automotive qualified; automotive (-Q) alternatives exist."
  }
 ],
